#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128878 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "76ff3ea8-e07c-5dba-a9af-a4f68b03fadb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5516" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$." :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2014/Source/Images/8e8b4e5e53d3ee1ee00e439e1bc6d2fe.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nOur goal is to compute $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$. First, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{B → C}^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/(1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-1))\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T]_{C} &= P_{B → C} \\cdot [T]_{B} \\cdot P_{B → C}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128879 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "4b287ac7-7569-59bc-a319-0e62996083bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5517" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$." :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2014/Source/Images/16fb4c0100b25b8254f7b585766682f1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{S}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}^{-1}\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{B → S}^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/(5 \\cdot 3 - (-7) \\cdot (-2))\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T]_{S} &= P_{B → S} \\cdot [T]_{B} \\cdot P_{B → S}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 13 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128908 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "5e4b6303-8ec5-5ba6-ba1d-3b28ff8924b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5897" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$." :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2014/Source/Images/c5e4593940236538bb9a20433d7b4e8d.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$. To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; c_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}c_{2} & b_{1}\\end{vmatrix}b_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{B → C}^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/(1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-1))\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T]_{C} &= P_{B → C} \\cdot [T]_{B} \\cdot P_{B → C}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "c269a1d0-a47c-5f5c-96d8-4da2ecd35a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8563" :question/problem "Let $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$, respectively. If $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $\\mathcal{C}$ to basis $\\mathcal{B}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}$\n2. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$\n3. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2014/Source/Images/f7d159aa878f5421b6005bd8da3c8d11.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$.\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2e2b12-6fb1-5d70-8061-6e4d0cc5ac5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127263} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ab0108-3bb2-51f1-b139-099a7ceee51f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127264} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf4d81b-6d53-5c58-a683-25399d0f5bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127265} {:answer/id #uuid "18c4fddb-bcb1-5a86-8164-8b51e5458ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127266} {:answer/id #uuid "535b2f52-557b-50b5-aab2-9e9271c4af8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f2e2b12-6fb1-5d70-8061-6e4d0cc5ac5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127263} :answer-field/id #uuid "e631224b-9b35-5821-b674-663ee77b3cea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127262}] :question/id #uuid "d9baad5b-5623-53e5-a4c6-324293957526" :question/math-academy-id "q-38356" :question/problem "$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{B → C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a26ea1f-ee44-587a-a448-6f486b3acf3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127275} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3b2d2c-0226-5569-897d-9f9b0d09e47b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127276} {:answer/id #uuid "cca81f9b-68fc-541c-b7b8-48ae8540d4a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127277} {:answer/id #uuid "40cb6dde-7047-570d-a0aa-916e6bc53b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127278} {:answer/id #uuid "5c837155-8ffd-558b-b04f-a812da0bffab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cb6dde-7047-570d-a0aa-916e6